    ax.set(xticks=x,xticklabels=labels,ylabel='Pair-common residual MSE (lower is better)',title='The head fits its training pairs but fails to generalize');ax.legend(fontsize=8)
    fig.savefig(diagnosis/'generalization.png',dpi=180);plt.close(fig)
    lines+=['','## Root-cause follow-up: read-only, zero additional GPU','','The latest failed recipe is not zero improvement on every example. It improves **276/3,072 pair-by-seed-by-horizon records (8.98%)**; these records reuse512pairs and are correlated. All six aggregate combinations worsen. Per-combination improvement counts are53,45,32,32,58,56 out of512. This distinction does not rescue the failed recipe.','','![Training versus held-out errors](../results/motion_scene_diagnosis/generalization.png)','','**Measured failure mechanism: overfitting.** Applying the already-fitted coefficients back to their fitting halves reduces common-residual MSE by48.9–54.9%. Applying those same coefficients to the other halves increases average error by39.9–46.8%. Median pair gains are negative in all six cells, so a few extreme examples are not the sole cause. The effective ridge degrees of freedom are122.8–123.4 with only256pairs per fit. The independent KKT audit and CPU/CUDA replay do not indicate a numerical-solver or residual-construction failure. These observations establish a generalization gap; degrees of freedom alone do not identify every cause.','','**Structural input mismatch:** the head receives128current-frame features and6action summaries, but neither historical observations nor the frozen predicted scene. During occlusion the generator paints the entire arena with the same shutter. Historical ball height, reference position and speed can change while the last four RGB frames and actions remain exactly identical. No deterministic current-frame encoder or current/action-only head can distinguish those controlled alternatives.','','![Identical current inputs, different futures](../results/motion_scene_diagnosis/observability.png)','','Changing one legal scene factor at a time (height, reference position, speed), across32training scene templates and2horizons, produced192/192witnesses with identical recent RGB/actions but different historical RGB and different pair-mean future RGB. This is a simulator observability proof, not a feature-level predictability experiment or a Phase1 pass. It does not quantify how much encoded position-null residual is caused by omitted geometry.','','The design assumption needing correction is **pair-common residual = current-observable background residual**. Averaging twins removes their antisymmetric difference; it does not remove scene-dependent future object structure. Similarly, a readout-null feature direction is invisible to one fixed linear position readout; it is not necessarily background or irrelevant information. The residual fraction reachable by the allowed null correction is80.1–85.1%, so a complete absence of output-space room is not the main explanation either.','','**What to change before another fit:** establish that remaining residuals are predictable from legal retained history or the frozen forecast, using paired cache diagnostics on the existing validation split. Any next head must receive the information required by its target and demonstrate held-out incremental benefit; simply making the current-only head bigger or running it longer is unsupported. Stronger regularization could reduce variance, but no alpha search was performed and it cannot restore absent information. No new repair is adopted here.','','CPU8500evaluated the existing12solutions only: no fitting, no model forward, no new validation/test access, no additionalGPU. Two compact files have matching native-before/local/native-after hashes; all3,072CSVrows independently reproduce the previous six crossfit means. The CPU allocation was cancelled and normal authenticated Sessions verifiedCompleted. All original acceptance thresholds and the fresh9137boundary remain unchanged.']
